Verdict
-------
MIXED.

By the butteraugli axis the buttloop IS a faithful proxy:
  - 11/12 cells |Δbfly| < 0.30 (NEG)
  - median |Δbfly| = 0.011 (≈ jxl-rs vs jxl-oxide cross-decoder FP noise)
  - the buttloop's perception of "how close are we to source?" matches
    what the user sees within noise on every cell EXCEPT 3637739 d=4

By the SSIM2 axis the buttloop is SYSTEMATICALLY PESSIMISTIC:
  - 3/12 cells |Δssim2| >= 2.0
  - mean signed Δssim2 = -1.31 (internal score consistently LOWER than
    decoded), max = -5.82 on 1531677 d=5
  - The buttloop's internal reconstruction (uniform-4 EPF sharpness,
    no patch subtraction in the recon pipeline, etc.) makes SSIM2
    measurements on the encoder side substantially worse than what
    the real decoder produces — because the production sharpness map
    + production EPF + production patch overlay improve SSIM2

Why MIXED, not clean either way:
  - The W44-116 root-cause memo was correct: the EPF-sharpness mismatch
    IS real (~0.05-0.17 R linear-RGB max-abs in pre-W44-117 conditions).
  - But: the W44-117 / W44-118 / W44-120 chain has been actively
    closing the GAP. The 11/12 cells with negligible bfly gap shows
    the gap is mostly fixed by the W44-117 family for bfly.
  - The SSIM2 gap is a DIFFERENT axis. SSIM2 weights luma structure
    more heavily; the buttloop never optimizes SSIM2 directly, so
    cells where the internal recon's structure differs from the
    decoded structure (via EPF / patches / splines) will read
    differently on SSIM2 even when bfly converges.
  - But: in EVERY case where SSIM2 differs substantially, the
    DECODED SSIM2 is HIGHER (better) than the internal — i.e. the
    real decoder produces better-quality output than the buttloop's
    target. This is NOT a quality bug — it's a metric divergence
    where the buttloop's local minimum on bfly produces SUPERIOR
    SSIM2 in the decoded output. P-1 (per-iter sharpness recompute)
    would NOT help here; it would either move the buttloop's
    convergence to a different point or just slow it down.

Implications for Phase 2 plan
-----------------------------
1. P-1 (per-iter EPF sharpness recompute) has NEGLIGIBLE BFLY EV
   on this 12-cell set. The buttloop's bfly target IS what users see.
   For 11/12 cells closing the +0.034 R max-abs at after_epf (the
   W44-138 wedge) would only marginally move bfly by < 0.30.
2. P-1 may have UNPREDICTABLE SSIM2 EV. The 3 CRIT-ssim2 cells show
   the decoder produces +2.8 to +5.8 BETTER ssim2 than the buttloop's
   internal recon. Per-iter sharpness recompute would change which
   point the buttloop converges to — not obviously toward improving
   SSIM2 in the decoded output. Could regress SSIM2-axis just as easily.
3. The 1 CRIT-bfly cell (3637739 d=4) DOES show a 0.56 bfly gap that
   would benefit from P-1. But it's an outlier. The W44-198 cluster
   handle on 3637739 may already address this via a different
   mechanism (content discriminator).
4. Decoded scores are at-or-better than cjxl on bfly (mean -0.39),
   roughly at-parity on SSIM2 (mean +0.05) across the 12-cell set —
   suggesting the encoder is converging well; what the buttloop
   thinks vs what users see has small EV on average.
5. The dominant remaining bfly gap (between us and cjxl, after
   decoding both) lives in cells the W44-99/100 + W44-198 cluster
   work is targeting — content-discriminator surface, not
   buttloop-recon-fidelity surface.

Recommended Phase 2 direction
-----------------------------
PIVOT to perf/architecture. P-1 has small bfly EV, ambiguous SSIM2
EV, and known 10-30% buttloop slowdown cost (per W44-116 estimate).

If a future chunk DOES want to revisit recon-vs-decoder fidelity:
  - Re-measure ONLY on the 3637739-class cells (CRIT-bfly).
  - Measure SSIM2 from a DIFFERENT proxy — e.g., per-block SSIM map
    on the internal recon vs decoded — to discriminate "different
    point, similar quality" from "worse point, worse quality".
  - The 1025469/1420710/1531677 d=5 CRIT-ssim2 cells should be
    re-examined for whether the buttloop's bfly-locally-optimal
    convergence is producing SSIM2-suboptimal final output that
    user-visible quality matrics catch.

What this DOES NOT rule out
---------------------------
- The W44-138 +0.034 R max-abs at after_epf on 1418519 d=5 is real
  (and was the original P-1 motivation), but A8 shows the resulting
  bfly delta is only -0.148 (NEG bucket). The +0.034 max-abs is
  a single-pixel outlier; it does not move bfly meaningfully.
- The W44-117 family of fixes (one-shot sharpness seed at iter-0)
  is doing its job — that's why 11/12 cells are NEG-bfly.
- SSIM2-targeted optimization remains a separate axis the buttloop
  doesn't address. The W44-105/107/108/109 qac-scale chain handles
  the cells the bfly-only optimization misses on SSIM2; those
  remain load-bearing per W44-119.

DO NOT (binding for future agents)
----------------------------------
1. DO NOT ship W44-138 P-1 (per-iter sharpness recompute) as a
   pure correctness fix. Measurement is conclusive: the
   recon-vs-decoder gap is bfly-negligible on 11/12 cells. The 1
   outlier needs targeted investigation, not a global mechanism.
2. DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for the SSIM2 divergence (per W44-66
   user correction).
3. DO NOT re-investigate `reconstruct_xyb`, `gab_smooth`, AFV IDCT,
   per-strategy IDCT, DC CfL, dequant_bias as candidate causes —
   all RULED OUT in W44-113/114/115/116. The bfly gap is just
   negligible NOW; the SSIM2 gap is a separate axis the buttloop
   doesn't optimize.
4. DO NOT respawn another HONEST-STOP measurement chunk on this
   exact 12-cell set. The 4-decoder × 4-score sweep is conclusive.

